  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Migraine without aura, episodic, with bouts of near-daily attacks (G43.009)</content>. Dr. Haddad confirms migraine without aura by ICHD-3 criteria: at least five attacks lasting 4 to 72 hours, of moderate to severe throbbing pain made worse by routine activity, with nausea and with light and noise sensitivity, and no better explanation. The long, stable history and normal examination without red flags mean no imaging is needed. With 13 migraine days in four weeks during bouts and a MIDAS of 39, preventive treatment is indicated. [S]<list><item>Sumatriptan 100 mg with naproxen 500 mg early in an attack, a second sumatriptan after two hours if needed.</item><item>Propranolol extended-release 80 mg each evening, to be raised to 120 mg at four weeks if tolerated.</item><item>Keep acute medicine to two days a week or fewer and record every dose in the diary.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Stiff right wrist after an old dislocation (M25.631)</content>. Writing remains slow and painful after an hour. [S]<list><item>Occupational therapy visit as planned; dictation software in the meantime.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Red-flag screen (SNNOOP10): systemic signs, neurological signs, sudden or new pattern, onset after 50, positional or Valsalva features, papilledema, progressive course</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No red flags; normal neurological examination and fundi</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Headache diary: headache and migraine days, attack features, triggers</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>14 days kept: headache on the first 7 days, all meeting migraine features; 13 migraine days in the past four weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Acute-medicine days (medication overuse headache limits: triptan fewer than 10, NSAID fewer than 15 days a month)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Naproxen on four days in 14; no combination analgesic since intake</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Acute treatment response (pain-free at two hours, return to work)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Naproxen gave partial relief, rarely pain-free at two hours; triptan added</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Cardiovascular screen before a triptan</td><td>Before the first triptan prescription</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No chest pain, vascular disease, uncontrolled blood pressure or smoking; blood pressure 122/78</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Propranolol tolerability: pulse, blood pressure, tiredness, exercise tolerance, wheeze, cold hands, sleep</td><td>Four weeks after starting or changing the dose, then each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Before starting: pulse 70, blood pressure 122/78; no asthma, heart block or depression</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Preventive efficacy (at least 50% fewer migraine days)</td><td>At two to three months on an adequate dose</td><td>Due</td><td>Propranolol started today; efficacy judged at the three-month review</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Disability (MIDAS, recorded as its five items, and HIT-6)</td><td>At baseline and after about three months of prevention</td><td>Up to date</td><td>MIDAS 39 (grade IV) and HIT-6 66 at intake; repeat at three months</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Mood (PHQ-9) and anxiety (GAD-7), including on a beta-blocker</td><td>At baseline, four to six weeks after starting a preventive that can affect mood, then at each review</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-9 6 and GAD-7 7 at intake; repeat four to six weeks after starting propranolol</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Neuroimaging</td><td>Only for red flags or a change in pattern</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Stable pattern for more than twenty years, normal examination, no red flags</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Sleep, meals, caffeine and work-stress plan</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Six hours of sleep, two cups of coffee, regular lunch</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Headache medicine consultation</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Haddad today</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Occupational therapy visit for the right wrist and writing</td><td>Within four weeks of intake</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Requested at intake; awaiting an appointment</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>A two-week headache diary that allows a firm diagnosis. Outcome by 2026-05-21: Diary of 14 days kept; ICHD-3 criteria for migraine without aura met; no red flags [S]</item><item>Monthly migraine days reduced by at least half within three months of starting prevention. In progress; target 2026-08-13 [S]</item><item>Acute medicine on no more than two days a week, so that medication overuse headache does not develop. In progress; target 2026-08-13 [S]</item><item>Pain-free within two hours of acute treatment in most attacks. In progress; target 2026-08-13 [S]</item><item>Lower headache-related disability. In progress; target 2026-08-13 [S]</item><item>Write for longer without wrist pain. In progress; target 2026-08-13 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Headache Classification Committee of the International Headache Society (IHS). The International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd edition (2018); The American Headache Society Consensus Statement: Update on integrating new migraine treatments into clinical practice (2021); Calcitonin gene-related peptide-targeting therapies are a first-line option for the prevention of migraine: An American Headache Society position statement update (2024); The American Headache Society Position Statement On Integrating New Migraine Treatments Into Clinical Practice (2019); Pharmacologic Treatments of Acute Episodic Migraine Headache in Outpatient Settings: A Clinical Guideline From the American College of Physicians (2025); Prevention of Episodic Migraine Headache Using Pharmacologic Treatments in Outpatient Settings: A Clinical Guideline From the American College of Physicians (2025); Choosing wisely in headache medicine: the American Headache Society&apos;s list of five things physicians and patients should question (2013)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Six-week review in four weeks. Diary continued. Call for a headache unlike his usual attacks, weakness, numbness, fever or a stiff neck. Next scheduled visit 2026-06-18: six-week review, four weeks after starting propranolol: tolerability, attacks, acute medicine days and the wrist. [S]</paragraph></text>

